# Notifications, acknowledgement and alerts

Say who a message is for, which channel carries it and what is actually known. Sending a notification, showing it, dismissing it and fixing the problem it describes are four separate events. Do not decide that someone saw a message because a request succeeded or a badge disappeared.

These examples are local previews with invented household values. They do not send a notification or control a home. Open an example to see it at full width or read its HTML.

## Scope and native ownership

A persistent notification appears in the Home Assistant frontend and stays until a person dismisses it. The `persistent_notification.create` and `persistent_notification.dismiss` actions add and remove one, and `persistent_notification.dismiss_all` removes all of them. Other integrations have their own delivery and action rules.

The Civica sidebar badge restyles the native count and adds the text “1 item needs attention” or “N items need attention” for assistive technology, and “99 or more” at the top of the count. It does not deliver messages, record that anyone read one or resolve a repair.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Request sent or accepted | The outcome the sending integration actually returned | Write Read or Delivered without evidence of it |
| Message shown in the frontend | A route to its details | Say which person read it |
| Acknowledged or dismissed | What was done and to what | Say the underlying condition is resolved |
| Resolved | The source's own condition, or the outcome of an explicit workflow | Treat a count of zero as proof the repair worked |
| Same condition repeated | Update the existing message where supported | Announce each sensor refresh as a new emergency |

## Integration contract

1. Choose the delivery route and the audience on purpose. A shared dashboard, a phone push message and a persistent notification are seen by different people and last for different times.
2. Map only the status fields the backend provides. If there is no delivery or read receipt, say so. Do not make one up from the response to an action.
3. Use a stable message identity when the integration lets you update a message. For a persistent notification, giving the same `notification_id` replaces the earlier one. Keep dismissal, acknowledgement and the state of the problem separate in the implementation.
4. An action button must keep the native permission checks and say what it will do. Do not present a command with consequences as an ordinary “View details” link.

## Accessible interaction

- Keep the message detail reachable through a named link. A number in a badge adds to it and must not be the only explanation.
- Announce a newly relevant outcome once, with live-region urgency that matches it. Keep a readable history where the native system provides one.
- Show a shorter preview on a shared screen without changing what the message means. Do not show private names, precise locations or security details by default in public examples. See [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](presence-and-privacy.html).

## Failure and recovery

- If sending fails, say which channel failed and whether the other channels have their own confirmed results.
- If an acknowledgement is not confirmed, keep that uncertainty and keep the original condition showing.
- For a problem that keeps coming back, do not raise its severity just because it keeps coming back. Use the source's own definition of urgency.

